00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:04.700
JOSEPH PICONE: Great.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.700
OK.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.290
Well, good morning or afternoon.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:11.170
We're really excited to have a chance to talk
data wrangling to people today.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.270
And we're really appreciative they're hosting
this seminar so that

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:16.040
we can get a chance to have this meeting.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:22.650
What I would like to do today is to talk mostly
about how important data wrangling is to this

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:28.659
whole area of machine learning and big data.

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:34.110
Data wrangling is defined as the process of
mapping data from an unstructured format to

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.639
another format that enables automated processing.

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:39.730
So that's a pretty vague statement I think.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:44.999
But today, what we're going to do is we're
going to focus on data wrangling of physiological

00:03:44.999 --> 00:03:52.799
signals because these pose some rather unique
challenges compared to text data or the other

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:57.269
types of data that you find in a lot of machine
learning applications.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:00.590
Signal data's always a bit more challenging
because of the nature of the signal.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:08.599
So we'll focus today on signal data, and particularly
we'll spend some time talking about EEGs.

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:11.840
And then the next talk will focus more on
the tech side.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.030
How we deal with medical reports, and how
we deal with unstructured text data, which

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:21.110
also plays a role in this because we need
both sources of data to actually do automated

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:24.640
data wrangling of these types of signals.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:32.790
So let's begin by talking a little bit about
big data, and machine learning.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:41.910
For many years now we've had a fairly well
defined iterative process to developing this

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:47.350
kind of technology, which we might call a
classic machine learning paradigm.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.500
It begins with collection of data.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:55.660
I hope if there's one message I'll leave you
with today it's how important data is to solving

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.760
these kinds of problems, because if the data
is not representative of the problem then

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:04.810
most of the research done is really not that
valuable.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:08.690
And making the data representative of the
problem is also much more challenging than

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:09.690
what people think.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:14.310
So we'll talk quite a bit today about data
and they associated issues with data, such

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:16.190
as annotation.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.010
But we begin with data.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:23.370
We typically select some features and we're
always worried, of course, about whether these

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:27.850
features capture meaningful differences between
the patterns.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.610
We then select some model.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.970
Or we might try many models.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:35.810
But we always worry, how do we find the best
model?

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:40.230
We'll then go through a training process where
we estimate the parameters of the model, and

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:43.300
try to optimize the model on the data.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:45.750
And then we'll evaluate the classifier.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:53.130
And if all goes well that evaluation will
give us insight into the data itself.

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:58.530
And very often we end up then recollecting,
or re-annotating the data and beginning the

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.250
process all over again.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:05.660
We'll also talk today a little bit about how
important evaluation metrics are to these

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:10.640
problems, and how important it is to make
sure that as you optimize the technology you're

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.520
optimizing it
on things that are meaningful to the end users

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.830
of the technology.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:21.460
But this is sort of a closed loop process
because we can iterate on this many times.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:28.770
And the technology today is sufficiently powerful
enough that if we train on the same data that

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:34.990
we evaluate on we can usually drive error
rates very close to zero, which is very satisfying.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:39.520
But that doesn't mean that the system necessarily
generalizes well to new data sets.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:43.550
So we'll talk a little bit about that problem
as well.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:48.670
Other things to think about, often when you
do these kinds of optimizations you end up

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:53.630
with technology which is very application
specific and data dependent.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:57.470
What's even worse though is that customers
or users don't often completely understand

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:03.090
the requirements, so very often after you
develop the first version of the system you

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:07.680
find out that that's not really what the customers
wanted, and then you iterate through several

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:08.680
times.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:12.750
In fact, on EEG data that we'll talk about,
we're actually on our fourth generation of

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:16.720
trying to make the system do something that's
relevant to the users.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:22.660
And it's been a learning process for the users,
as well as for the technology developers.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:26.300
And data collection is often an ongoing process
that drives the technology.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:34.170
And usually we like to collect data that allows
us to solve specific problems.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:36.590
But you really can't do that all at once.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:41.370
So every year we try to collect data that
pushes the technology a little further.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.470
And we've had an evolution like that in fields
like speech and image processing.

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:50.680
We're just beginning that process in areas
like bioengineering, particularly areas like

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:55.350
EEGs, and such.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:59.030
So why is big data so important?

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:04.930
I think many of you have probably seen a lot
of press in recent years about the progress

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:10.610
that's been made on some very visible tasks,
such as speech recognition.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:18.550
In the past 5 years alone we've seen significant
improvement and performance of these systems

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:21.030
based on what are called, "deep learning systems."

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:25.310
It's been a very, very exciting, very active
time because of this.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:32.130
To put this in perspective, on tasks which
we were scoring 80% error rate-- that's 80%

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:35.620
error rate in the 1990s.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:42.190
This is a task based on conversational speech
recognition, today we're scoring error rates

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:44.510
in the range of about 7%.

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:49.649
The performance on these tasks is getting
very close to human performance and that didn't

00:08:49.649 --> 00:08:50.649
happen overnight.

00:08:50.649 --> 00:08:54.649
However that took about 50 years of research
to get to that point.

00:08:54.649 --> 00:08:59.329
But progress has been significant in the last
5 years, and that's primarily been due to

00:08:59.329 --> 00:09:03.670
the emergence of deep learning systems, coupled
with the availability of large amounts of

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:04.670
data.

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:05.670
systems, coupled with the availability of
large amounts of data.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:09.170
So this is a slide I want people to really
pay attention to.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:12.819
That deep learning systems require large amounts
of data.

00:09:12.819 --> 00:09:17.290
We can get very good performance with these
systems but we need lots of data.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:22.870
And this is one of a number of examples that
has been cited in recent years.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:28.610
Here we have a task where we compare what
was conventional technology maybe 10 years

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:29.610
ago.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:34.779
Most of the commercial systems were based
on a technology called hidden Markov models.

00:09:34.779 --> 00:09:37.660
And here we have a deep learning system.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:43.749
And on a fairly moderate amount of data you
very often see that these deep learning systems

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:47.610
perform comparably to the existing technology.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:52.699
But you don't see big wins with these systems
because these deep learning systems have many,

00:09:52.699 --> 00:09:57.860
many parameters associated with them, and
they take a long time to train on the data,

00:09:57.860 --> 00:09:59.550
and you just need lots of data.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.880
However, when we do have lots of data-- so
when we go from, for example, about 10 hours

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:09.699
of speech data, to about 2000 hours of speech
data, we begin to see very significant differences

00:10:09.699 --> 00:10:12.839
in performance between the 2.

00:10:12.839 --> 00:10:17.699
So that's good news in some sense, because
it says if we have adequate resources to develop

00:10:17.699 --> 00:10:21.740
the technology we can get very, very good
performance.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:27.950
But this is a very sobering issue for biomedical
or bioengineering applications because it's

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:34.060
very rare that such applications have the
equivalent of 2000 hours of speech data.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:37.839
And I'll talk a little bit about that in a
moment.

00:10:37.839 --> 00:10:39.779
Big data's everywhere.

00:10:39.779 --> 00:10:44.880
A couple favorite quotes of ours in the speech
research community, which I've worked in for

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:51.579
many years-- we actually, back in the mid
80s a researcher at IBM, Bob Mercer, coined

00:10:51.579 --> 00:10:55.339
a phrase, which has been very popular, which
is, "There's no data like more data."

00:10:55.339 --> 00:11:00.779
With new data you always learn new things,
and you always worry about the ability of

00:11:00.779 --> 00:11:05.670
your system to generalize so it's always good.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:09.029
It never hurts to have more data.

00:11:09.029 --> 00:11:13.180
More recently you've heard a lot about big
data, and here's one of the more famous quotes

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:16.149
about big data.

00:11:16.149 --> 00:11:21.319
Everybody talks about it, but in reality,
when you start looking at the data sets available

00:11:21.319 --> 00:11:27.360
in the community for various bioengineering
tests you very often find that these sets

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:28.360
are small.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:33.589
They may consist of tens of subjects, or even
a hundred of subjects, but they don't consist

00:11:33.589 --> 00:11:40.000
of thousands, or tens of thousands, or millions,
that we need to train these big data systems.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:47.700
And that's something that we're attempting
to change.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.300
Why are we so interested in this?

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:53.819
Well there is a belief that if we can actually
build systems that look over large amounts

00:11:53.819 --> 00:11:58.819
of data we can develop new science, we can
uncover new

00:11:58.819 --> 00:12:04.930
relationships and dependencies, or we can
improve the ability of systems to diagnose.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:09.550
And I want to come back to that issue because
that's an interesting issue when we talk about

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:16.430
areas like EEGs or pathology that require
significant amounts of expertise.

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:18.260
So where do we get this kind of data?

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:23.180
Well, in commercially viable applications
there are significant economic motivation.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:28.040
So in an application like speech recognition
where billions of dollars are being invested

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:32.660
in development of the technology there's a
good justification for creating large data

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:34.009
resources.

00:12:34.009 --> 00:12:39.250
And to some extent, since everybody understands
language, we all use it every day, the data

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:41.050
is quite abundant.

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:45.630
In bioengineering applications, however, the
commercial opportunities are not so significant.

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:48.009
It's not to say that they're not important.

00:12:48.009 --> 00:12:55.990
They're of course, very important, but when
you start dealing with financial issues and

00:12:55.990 --> 00:13:02.010
such there's less willingness to invest large
amounts of money in the collection of data.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:05.430
So the development of data resources is lagging.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:09.209
However there is enormous amounts of data
available in the health care industry.

00:13:09.209 --> 00:13:14.209
So I'll talk today about a database we've
been building over the past 4 years, which

00:13:14.209 --> 00:13:17.559
is known as a [? TOAGEG ?] Corpus.

00:13:17.559 --> 00:13:23.649
And it essentially consists of the last 14
years of EEGs collected at Temple Hospital.

00:13:23.649 --> 00:13:25.660
We're now at about 13,000 sessions.

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.019
Over 16,000 patients.

00:13:28.019 --> 00:13:32.500
It's a very rich, very interesting database
because it's pure clinical data.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.470
It's not collected under controlled conditions.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:37.749
It's very real data.

00:13:37.749 --> 00:13:42.889
There's a lot of this data, if we count video
and such, the size is staggering.

00:13:42.889 --> 00:13:50.310
But we're able to-- we use about a terabyte
worth of disk space to process the experiments

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:52.980
that I'll talk about it a little bit later
today.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:59.309
If I take another example we've recently become
interested in digital pathology.

00:13:59.309 --> 00:14:01.639
Every hospital has a department of pathology.

00:14:01.639 --> 00:14:05.319
Every department of pathology reads lots of
slides every day.

00:14:05.319 --> 00:14:10.350
We estimate that there may be as many as 1
million pathology slides read each day.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:16.540
So if there was a way to tap into that data
we could build very, very large databases

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:20.040
that would fuel the development of these deep
learning systems.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:25.300
But, of course, tapping into that data is
a bit of a challenge because of IRB and HIPPA

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:26.300
concerns.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:30.480
And that's really one of the reasons we see
data wrangling become so important.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:36.329
There's different facets of the data wrangling
problem but creating a pipeline where the

00:14:36.329 --> 00:14:42.040
data from these clinical applications can
flow directly into research is very important,

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:46.649
because then, very quickly, we can generate
the vast amounts of data that we need to train

00:14:46.649 --> 00:14:51.069
these systems.

00:14:51.069 --> 00:14:52.980
Why is big data crucial?

00:14:52.980 --> 00:15:00.329
Well, it's very easy to take a very large
database, and filter through it

00:15:00.329 --> 00:15:02.230
to find that it's actually very small.

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:07.810
So when we look at specific patient histories,
even in the EEG corpus that we collected,

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:13.480
which is over 16,000 patients, when you look
at patients with specific medical conditions

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:18.879
you start to find out that a very small number
of subjects in the database actually have

00:15:18.879 --> 00:15:21.519
those conditions, or share those conditions.

00:15:21.519 --> 00:15:26.709
So even though the data starts to be very
large one of the interesting things about

00:15:26.709 --> 00:15:33.860
bioengineering applications is that the subjects
are so unique that when you start to do specific

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:39.959
studies on the data these large databases
begin to look very small.

00:15:39.959 --> 00:15:43.399
And this is something we're seeing, for example,
with seizures.

00:15:43.399 --> 00:15:48.189
Even though we have a large number of EEG
sessions in our data, the number of sessions

00:15:48.189 --> 00:15:52.459
in which patients actually experience seizures
is not as large as you might think, and is

00:15:52.459 --> 00:16:00.189
actually small on the small side for developing
this kind of technology.

00:16:00.189 --> 00:16:06.259
But what's more interesting is that clinicians
often see a small percentage of the total

00:16:06.259 --> 00:16:07.949
population throughout their career.

00:16:07.949 --> 00:16:13.069
And a lot of their ability to read and interpret
this data is based on the patients that they've

00:16:13.069 --> 00:16:14.870
seen over the years.

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:20.040
And one of the reasons I think big data's
really interesting is very few clinicians

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:26.470
will see the full range of diseases and pathologies
in their particular discipline.

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:31.149
They tend to see a subset that's indicative
of the type of patients that they see.

00:16:31.149 --> 00:16:36.189
And when they then encounter patients outside
of those norms they also have trouble interpreting

00:16:36.189 --> 00:16:38.079
this information.

00:16:38.079 --> 00:16:43.869
So it's really important that with big data
we can look across large populations, and

00:16:43.869 --> 00:16:48.639
I think, actually build systems that may ultimately
exceed human performance because they can

00:16:48.639 --> 00:16:55.540
see a much wider range of conditions than
your average clinician might see.

00:16:55.540 --> 00:17:03.180
So let's look specifically at 2 applications,
EEGs-- EEgs are used to diagnose a variety

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:08.269
of brain related illnesses, and the process
that's done today is actually very similar

00:17:08.269 --> 00:17:10.299
to the way it was done 50 years ago.

00:17:10.299 --> 00:17:12.970
An EEG is recorded for a period of time.

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:16.470
That can range from 20 minutes to several
days.

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:22.549
And then a clinician sits down and reads those
EEGs, and looks for evidence of seizures,

00:17:22.549 --> 00:17:24.610
and things like that.

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:30.000
About 93% of these short term EEGs are inconclusive
so patients often have to come back to the

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.919
hospital several times, or be under observation
for long periods of time before neurologists

00:17:35.919 --> 00:17:41.080
can see the types of things they need to see
to properly diagnose the patient.

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:46.010
Manual interpretation of an EEG is performed
by a board certified neurologist.

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:49.710
That means they usually have to go to med
school.

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:53.350
They'll usually do a residency after that.

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:55.630
And then they'll often do a fellowship after
that.

00:17:55.630 --> 00:18:00.140
So before they become board certified you're
talking about many years of training.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:05.980
And that's why there's a shortage of people
who can read and interpret EEGS, which presents

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:12.110
a great opportunity for automated technologies
like we're discussing today.

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:17.320
But EEG signals themselves are very challenging
to interpret.

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:22.940
Neurologists usually do this by visually inspecting
the waveforms, and looking for specific events

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:26.740
that they think are an indication of abnormalities.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:36.519
But not surprisingly the interrater agreement
on these localized events is actually very

00:18:36.519 --> 00:18:37.519
poor.

00:18:37.519 --> 00:18:40.000
They may not catch every event.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:42.110
They may not mark every event.

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:44.909
Certainly there's general agreement on a diagnosis.

00:18:44.909 --> 00:18:50.919
At a high level they obviously diagnose the
patients properly, but at a low level there's

00:18:50.919 --> 00:18:53.870
a lot of room for interpretation of these
events.

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:57.910
And that's what makes this a very challenging
problem from a machine learning standpoint,

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:02.760
is we're actually trying to replicate the
craft that neurologists use to read these

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:03.860
and interpret these EEGs.

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:07.760
And it's very difficult to codify that with
algorithms.

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:12.549
So deep learning, again, provides a very interesting
opportunity for us, in that we hope these

00:19:12.549 --> 00:19:18.840
algorithms are capable of automatically learning
some of this information from the signal rather

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:22.970
than having to build an expert system that
tries to replicate the way neurologists actually

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:27.260
do this.

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:32.190
If we look at a similar application, surgical
pathology, this is an application that's beginning

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:38.010
to pick up a lot of momentum in the community,
because it's viewed as a nice application

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.590
of machine learning technology.

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:45.350
It's a very big operation, because every hospital
that does surgery essentially has a pathology

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:48.330
lab running 7 by 24.

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:55.330
It takes a significant amount of schooling
to be certified to interpret these pathology

00:19:55.330 --> 00:20:03.049
slides, and therefore, again, there's a shortage
of people who can do this.

00:20:03.049 --> 00:20:05.279
The benefits of this are obvious.

00:20:05.279 --> 00:20:07.570
We can do remote diagnosis.

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:16.340
We can automate this process, and handle things
much more efficiently than having neurologists

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:19.799
on call 24 hours a day.

00:20:19.799 --> 00:20:23.889
But it's also, like many of these problems,
somewhat subtle.

00:20:23.889 --> 00:20:29.789
There's a large number of cases, you could
say, that are sort of textbook-ish, but it's

00:20:29.789 --> 00:20:36.769
not uncommon that a neurologist will call
in other neurologists when they see something

00:20:36.769 --> 00:20:41.340
that they're not familiar with, or is outside
of the norm of what they're used to seeing

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:45.860
on an everyday basis.

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:50.070
So what we're going to talk about next for
a few slides is this problem of real time

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:52.230
automatic interpretation of EEG.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:57.470
So what that means, in a nutshell, is you
take a signal, and you automatically label

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:01.700
it for events, which are deemed to be important
by neurologists.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:07.309
So here we're showing the ability to label
particular channels with events, and then

00:21:07.309 --> 00:21:13.169
we can also make some kind of summary judgment
about the event.

00:21:13.169 --> 00:21:15.750
The types of things you look for can very.

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:17.340
We'll talk a little bit about seizures.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:23.650
And we'll talk a little bit about signals,
like spikes.

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:32.080
Real time seizure detection is a very big
need in this area because very often patients

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:40.779
will be under observation in an ICU, or an
epilepsy monitoring unit, and they'll be there

00:21:40.779 --> 00:21:46.070
for long periods of time, and they'll be under
constant observation, but neurologists won't

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:50.299
be available to actually read and interpret
the EEGs in real time.

00:21:50.299 --> 00:21:58.529
So there's a great opportunity for real time
detection because that will allow neurologists

00:21:58.529 --> 00:22:02.059
to better treat their patients, because they're
getting notified more quickly when seizure

00:22:02.059 --> 00:22:04.130
events, and things like that occur.

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:06.290
But detection of seizures is very challenging.

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:12.510
And most of the commercial technology today
false alarms at such a high rate that neurologists

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:15.940
and clinicians simply turn off the real time
alerting.

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:22.240
And this is a problem that's gained a lot
of interest in the past few years because

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:27.299
there's so many automated systems in hospitals
producing real time alerts, and so many of

00:22:27.299 --> 00:22:32.019
them false alarm at such a high rate that
physicians and care providers essentially

00:22:32.019 --> 00:22:34.909
ignore all of these real time alerts.

00:22:34.909 --> 00:22:40.429
So if you can do seizure detection reliably
with a low false alarm rate that would be

00:22:40.429 --> 00:22:44.419
a very significant advance in the field.

00:22:44.419 --> 00:22:48.200
What things we're trying to do in this context,
we're trying to identify the start and stop

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:50.090
time of the signal.

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:54.570
We're trying to identify which channels it
occurred on so we can localize that to the

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.480
portion of the brain where it's occurring.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:04.340
And then we try to identify a few other things
like the type of seizure that's occurring.

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:09.160
Seizure detection using surface EEG recordings
is extremely challenging because the signals

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:16.289
are very noisy, and real time clinical applications
demand an extremely low false alarm rate.

00:23:16.289 --> 00:23:23.070
This is much below what current machine learning
systems are capable of but ideally they would

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:31.010
like one false alarm every eight hours, which
is a very, very aggressive goal.

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:35.909
When we talk about other types of events that
we'd like to automatically label in the signal,

00:23:35.909 --> 00:23:38.081
here's an example of some of them.

00:23:38.081 --> 00:23:41.980
Spikes and sharp waves, GPEDS, PLEDs, and
such.

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:45.670
We also have a number of artifacts that we
have to be able to ignore.

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:47.029
Things like eye movements.

00:23:47.029 --> 00:23:54.590
And building algorithms to do this requires
the signals be labeled with these kinds of

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:57.680
events,
but of course, if there's poor going interrater

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:02.251
agreement amongst experts on when these events
are occurring, that creates a problem that

00:24:02.251 --> 00:24:07.230
you don't have the gold standard annotations
of the data that you need to produce high

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:10.380
performance systems.

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:14.190
Clinical data contains many artifacts that
can be easily interpreted as spikes.

00:24:14.190 --> 00:24:17.529
This is why false alarm rates are so high
with some of these systems.

00:24:17.529 --> 00:24:21.320
Manually reviewing the data is time consuming.

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:27.600
And in ICU applications the need to analyze
the data in real time is very important.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:33.889
Performance requirements for this application,
ideally, would be detection rates above 95%,

00:24:33.889 --> 00:24:36.669
and false alarm rates below 5%.

00:24:36.669 --> 00:24:41.820
And we do have technology which is approaching
those levels of performance now.

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:45.159
That's one of the things we've been able to
build over the past few years.

00:24:45.159 --> 00:24:51.269
So we think the technology is getting to the
point where it's clinically [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:51.269 --> 00:24:52.309
So how do we do this?

00:24:52.309 --> 00:25:00.009
Well most machine learning systems go through
a fairly standard process of converting the

00:25:00.009 --> 00:25:03.610
signal to some kind of feature description.

00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:10.960
We then use those that system then to spot--
we do a first pass at decoding where we spot

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:18.390
events, then we apply a second pass of decoding,
which attempts to look at sequences of those

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:22.940
events, and throw out the outliers, do a little
bit of smoothing, a little bit of post-processing,

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:26.909
and then we do a 3rd level of post-processing.

00:25:26.909 --> 00:25:28.320
Sometimes these 2 levels are combined.

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:32.750
But the general idea is you take these raw
event outputs, and you try to post process

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:41.139
them with a system that's capable of understanding
the context in which these events occur, and

00:25:41.139 --> 00:25:43.889
interpreting them more accurately.

00:25:43.889 --> 00:25:48.269
The current system that we've developed, which
operates close to human performance on this

00:25:48.269 --> 00:25:51.669
task, uses a mixture of hidden Markov models,
and deep learning.

00:25:51.669 --> 00:25:57.210
We're in the process replacing the hidden
Markov model portion of that with a deep learning

00:25:57.210 --> 00:25:58.210
component.

00:25:58.210 --> 00:26:04.620
But generally speaking, [INAUDIBLE] the size
data sets that we have the performance differences

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:09.220
between most of these different types of machine
learning systems is pretty small.

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:15.899
It really is more a function of the definition
of the problem itself, and how the data is

00:26:15.899 --> 00:26:19.870
annotated, in terms of the performance that
you measure.

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:23.380
Arguing about what's a seizure, what's not
a seizure, what's an important or relevant

00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:25.899
seizure, and so forth.

00:26:25.899 --> 00:26:28.890
So let's now begin to talk a little bit about
data wrangling.

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:31.549
So we want to build a system that's able to
do this.

00:26:31.549 --> 00:26:37.820
And in a perfect world we would have data
that was annotated at a very fine level so

00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:42.590
that we could build what are called supervised
machine learning systems.

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:47.130
Systems that learn how to map data to the
answers that you want, and you've got very

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:50.659
high quality annotations that allow you to
do that.

00:26:50.659 --> 00:26:55.649
unfortunately there's a whole slew of issues
with this kind of data that have to be dealt

00:26:55.649 --> 00:26:58.429
with before you can get to that point.

00:26:58.429 --> 00:27:03.779
So the easy stuff-- we like to say, sort of,
this easy stuff-- first and foremost we have

00:27:03.779 --> 00:27:05.880
an anonymization issue.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:10.130
As many of you probably know in dealing with
health care related data, you have to preserve

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:13.399
the anonymity of the patients, and such.

00:27:13.399 --> 00:27:16.760
With some data that's relatively simple.

00:27:16.760 --> 00:27:22.139
With text data, for example, you can remove
patients names, and patient information if

00:27:22.139 --> 00:27:24.600
the data is structured, and things like that.

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:26.389
With some data that's very difficult.

00:27:26.389 --> 00:27:36.909
We have, for example, videos of patients,
which while they're EMU or ICU, under observation

00:27:36.909 --> 00:27:41.330
with the EEGs, in addition to collecting the
EEGs, they also collect video.

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:45.620
Well we can't really-anonymizing the videos
be very expensive, and therefore we can't

00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:47.220
really release the video.

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:55.210
Yet we know that neurologists use both video
cues, and signal cues to determine if a patient

00:27:55.210 --> 00:27:57.009
is having a seizure.

00:27:57.009 --> 00:28:00.640
But anonymization of the video is virtually
impossible.

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:04.341
Anonymization of the signal data is not so
bad.

00:28:04.341 --> 00:28:09.409
We can develop scripts to, and processing
systems to do that.

00:28:09.409 --> 00:28:12.090
But it's also time consuming.

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:17.890
With our EEG data, for example, we've had
to manually go through each and every EEG

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:24.830
report and remove names, and things like that,
so that the data is anonymous.

00:28:24.830 --> 00:28:27.320
That's a very time consuming process.

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:31.059
We can [AUDIO OUT] some of it in an automated
way with natural language processing.

00:28:31.059 --> 00:28:38.059
But just to be on the safe side we have to
manually review every piece of text data before

00:28:38.059 --> 00:28:41.740
it's released into the public domain.

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:46.470
But that's, sort of, I would say, in the scheme
of things a relatively easy part of the problem.

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:52.830
Then it gets more interesting when you talk
about the signal data itself.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:55.620
So, EEGs are a multichannel signal.

00:28:55.620 --> 00:29:00.909
Electrodes are placed around the head, and
you collect the electrical signals associated

00:29:00.909 --> 00:29:02.429
with these electrodes.

00:29:02.429 --> 00:29:07.399
But, of course, there's not a single configuration
that people use, and there's not a single

00:29:07.399 --> 00:29:10.110
way in which that configuration is administered.

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:15.610
There's actually several ways in which they
administer EEGs, including what they use as

00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:18.190
a reference point for voltages.

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:23.529
Whether they-- difference in signals, and
so forth.

00:29:23.529 --> 00:29:29.250
Neurologists, when they view the data, prefer
to view the data in a differential mode where

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:34.960
they subtract the voltages collected from
2 channels.

00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:39.279
And there are several montages which are popular.

00:29:39.279 --> 00:29:44.289
One of the ones that they like to use for
seizures is a montage called the TCP montage.

00:29:44.289 --> 00:29:49.389
So in addition to the raw data, they will
often not view the raw data directly, but

00:29:49.389 --> 00:29:57.419
they'll view differences in the channels,
and there's lots of variance to these things.

00:29:57.419 --> 00:30:02.820
Even something simple, like the channel labels
are not standard across institutions.

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:07.740
So it's very difficult sometimes to even tell
which channel corresponds to which electrode.

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:11.900
And that can
vary from technician, that can vary by hospital,

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:18.230
and any kind of automated data wrangling process
has to somehow comprehend that.

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:22.169
The channel identity is important because
it does allow us to spatially localize events.

00:30:22.169 --> 00:30:27.970
So the technology needs to be robust to all
these types of operational variations.

00:30:27.970 --> 00:30:33.259
And we can do some things to automate this.

00:30:33.259 --> 00:30:34.540
And we're having some success.

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:38.780
For example, at operating without the need
to use a montage.

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:44.679
We can do some of the filtering and such fairly
automatically.

00:30:44.679 --> 00:30:48.370
We can pair up channels fairly well.

00:30:48.370 --> 00:31:00.620
But very often the voltage reference points
used to collect the EEGs play a pretty significant

00:31:00.620 --> 00:31:02.970
role in the data itself.

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:05.460
And that's a little bit hard to adapt to automatically.

00:31:05.460 --> 00:31:13.559
So another solution is to build models for
each of these configurations that you expect

00:31:13.559 --> 00:31:14.559
to see.

00:31:14.559 --> 00:31:16.700
And that's often what a lot of the commercial
providers do.

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:21.549
Is they just have systems that are tuned to
the particular configurations that their customers

00:31:21.549 --> 00:31:25.130
are using.

00:31:25.130 --> 00:31:26.870
And then there's also artifact removal.

00:31:26.870 --> 00:31:31.110
There's a lot of different artifacts that
occur, because the signals are so low in voltage,

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:35.509
and they're so sensitive, you often get a
lot of artifacts in the data.

00:31:35.509 --> 00:31:44.220
Whether that's due to things like patient
movements, or electrical issues, like grounding

00:31:44.220 --> 00:31:50.120
and such, or electrical issues like 60 cycle
noise, and things.

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:51.770
You have to remove all of these.

00:31:51.770 --> 00:31:58.769
Otherwise your algorithms are going to false
alarm on these events, and call them seizure

00:31:58.769 --> 00:32:04.639
type events So there's a fair amount of pre-processing
that goes into the data, and that's often

00:32:04.639 --> 00:32:11.749
done through a mixture of filtering, and in
some cases, more sophisticated noise reduction

00:32:11.749 --> 00:32:13.289
signal processing.

00:32:13.289 --> 00:32:19.529
We actually build specific models of these
events, such as eye movements, and things

00:32:19.529 --> 00:32:25.299
and as part of the pattern recognition process
we identify those as eye movements and then

00:32:25.299 --> 00:32:27.149
we're able to ignore them.

00:32:27.149 --> 00:32:34.070
That's a much better strategy than trying
to build more classic systems that try to

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:38.889
filter these signals, or try to operate on
the waveforms with some kind of heuristics

00:32:38.889 --> 00:32:40.559
to remove these types of artifacts.

00:32:40.559 --> 00:32:44.990
But most commercial systems do a fair amount
of artifact removal, and the neurologists

00:32:44.990 --> 00:32:51.450
rely on that to be done before they begin
to interpret that EEGs.

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:56.370
So let's next talk a little bit about feature
selection.

00:32:56.370 --> 00:33:03.610
Most EEGs are analyzed based on waveforms,
but there has been a growing use of frequency

00:33:03.610 --> 00:33:06.320
domain visualizations in recent years.

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:11.440
In areas like speech recognition we've used
frequency domain representations for many,

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:15.450
many years, going back all the way to the
1950s and 60s.

00:33:15.450 --> 00:33:21.499
It's interesting that it's just
beginning to appear in the EEG community.

00:33:21.499 --> 00:33:25.950
So here I'm showing actually the onset of
a stroke.

00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:31.419
And we have the wave forms as well as the
frequency domain representations of that.

00:33:31.419 --> 00:33:38.280
And if you look closely enough at the spectra
you can see the EEGs emerging in areas, emerging

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:40.740
in the signal about here.

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:46.490
So one when you're developing features one
of the first things you have to ask is, where

00:33:46.490 --> 00:33:50.330
does the information manifest itself?

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:56.529
And in the early days of digital signal processing
we would spend a lot of time trying to develop

00:33:56.529 --> 00:34:03.409
algorithms that focused on the specific areas
of the time domain signal, or the frequency

00:34:03.409 --> 00:34:10.290
domain signals, to extract the kind of information
we thought was relevant in this process.

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:18.190
So for example in speech recognition systems
and in image recognition systems we use a

00:34:18.190 --> 00:34:21.919
combination of frequency domain features,
and time domain features.

00:34:21.919 --> 00:34:27.960
In speech recognition, for many years, we
used what's known as a filter based approach,

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:30.220
based on some kestral features.

00:34:30.220 --> 00:34:35.320
And we also threw in time domain energy, and
then we took those features, and differentiated

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:41.109
them twice, and built a fairly large feature
vector that contained estimates of the time

00:34:41.109 --> 00:34:46.990
domain signal, estimates of the frequency
domain signal, and derivatives and so forth.

00:34:46.990 --> 00:34:50.670
In EEGs we actually can do something similar.

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:56.149
We actually use a very similar filter bank
approach with EEGs because we know that different

00:34:56.149 --> 00:35:02.119
frequency ranges correspond to different activities
in the brain.

00:35:02.119 --> 00:35:07.150
And we actually use a front end that's very
similar to what we used in speech recognition

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:10.050
based on kestral features, and filter banks.

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:15.060
But deep learning systems are increasingly
performing this kind of analysis within the

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:16.830
neural network framework itself.

00:35:16.830 --> 00:35:23.650
And this has been one of the more exciting
aspects of this research, is that we now have

00:35:23.650 --> 00:35:30.829
deep learning systems that take as input samples
of the signal, or pixel values from the images,

00:35:30.829 --> 00:35:36.980
and the feature extraction process is actually
being done inside the deep learning system.

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:39.800
So this is a process which is known as a stacked
auto-encoder.

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:43.510
There's, of course, several variants of these.

00:35:43.510 --> 00:35:48.820
But the idea is that we use a hierarchy of
artificial neural networks.

00:35:48.820 --> 00:35:53.750
And these networks are trained on the data
so that they learn features that are very

00:35:53.750 --> 00:35:59.690
specific to the data, and they actually learn--
you could say, in a sense, they optimize the

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:00.690
features.

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:04.070
An advantage of this approach is that the
features are optimized as part of the training

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:10.861
process, so I don't have to invent or deduce
some kind of mathematical mapping of the signal

00:36:10.861 --> 00:36:12.140
to features.

00:36:12.140 --> 00:36:18.330
The system actually learns as part of the
training process the optimal way to do that.

00:36:18.330 --> 00:36:25.540
In fact, the system can learn how to both
represent and discriminate with the features.

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:30.540
You can do, for example, discriminative training
of features as a natural part of how we do

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:34.430
this inside of a deep learning system.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:38.569
The learning process is also unsupervised
so we don't necessarily need annotated data

00:36:38.569 --> 00:36:39.569
for this.

00:36:39.569 --> 00:36:45.300
So we can process large amounts of data through
this process, and we can arrive at features

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:50.000
which work pretty well across large populations.

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:57.390
And we really didn't have to do any manual
interpretation of the data.

00:36:57.390 --> 00:37:02.140
This can all be done in an automatic way so
you can transfer this kind of a concept from

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:07.550
domain to domain, or application to application,
without the need for a lot of expert knowledge

00:37:07.550 --> 00:37:09.380
on those specific signals.

00:37:09.380 --> 00:37:13.750
So this is a really good thing from an automated
data wrangling point of view, because I don't

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:19.530
need to have expertise in each discipline
that I'm trying to use this kind of modeling

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:22.480
technology for.

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:31.829
So the feature extraction is certainly one
of the problems that is fairly well in hand

00:37:31.829 --> 00:37:33.460
with this kind of technology.

00:37:33.460 --> 00:37:37.330
Now, as I mentioned at the beginning of the
talk, one of the things that's really happened

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:43.200
in the field in recent years is that these
so-called, "deep learning" approaches have

00:37:43.200 --> 00:37:49.280
been really making vast inroads in terms of
providing state of the art performance.

00:37:49.280 --> 00:37:56.869
Deep neural networks are essentially similar,
in concept, to the neural networks that were

00:37:56.869 --> 00:38:04.010
used many, many years ago but they have many
more layers, and they have many more nodes.

00:38:04.010 --> 00:38:09.020
The architectures themselves have been slowly
evolving now that we have the ability to train

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:10.230
and develop these systems.

00:38:10.230 --> 00:38:14.780
So one particular network that's very popular
these days is the bi-directional long short

00:38:14.780 --> 00:38:18.619
term memory nets, or BLSTM as they're called.

00:38:18.619 --> 00:38:24.280
And this is one of the networks that seems
to be delivering state of the art performance.

00:38:24.280 --> 00:38:26.750
There's many proposed architectures for these
systems.

00:38:26.750 --> 00:38:34.460
There are probably, easily, 50 popular architectures
out there today, and each one seems to work

00:38:34.460 --> 00:38:40.570
well on a specific set of applications, but
the one that works best for a particular applications

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:42.500
seems to be very application dependent.

00:38:42.500 --> 00:38:48.440
Though BLSTM seems to be working well across
speech, and video, and a few other types of

00:38:48.440 --> 00:38:50.640
signals like that.

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:53.080
These systems contain many levels in many
nodes.

00:38:53.080 --> 00:38:58.290
We're talking about millions of parameters.

00:38:58.290 --> 00:39:00.619
Tens of millions of degrees of freedom.

00:39:00.619 --> 00:39:03.660
And that is one of the reasons
why we need large amounts of data to train

00:39:03.660 --> 00:39:10.110
these systems, because they're very, very
large, in terms of the parameter counts.

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:14.880
For many years we envisioned these systems
but we really could not get them to train

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:16.059
properly.

00:39:16.059 --> 00:39:23.619
And that has been a significant obstacle until
about 2005, in the past decade, or so, techniques

00:39:23.619 --> 00:39:31.310
emerged that allowed these deep networks to
be trained properly, and to reach good solutions.

00:39:31.310 --> 00:39:35.760
And that's really when deep learning started
to take off, about the mid-2000s.

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:42.020
Training techniques began to emerge that allowed
these deep networks to actually converge to

00:39:42.020 --> 00:39:44.960
proper solutions.

00:39:44.960 --> 00:39:48.790
We used, in training systems an iterative,
or gradient descent approach.

00:39:48.790 --> 00:39:55.350
So these systems will typically be trained
through thousands of iterations, and that's

00:39:55.350 --> 00:40:00.300
a very computationally expensive process.

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:04.380
Since these systems have so many degrees of
freedom they need large amounts of data.

00:40:04.380 --> 00:40:08.880
Otherwise they have a tendency to over train
So on small data sets these kinds of very

00:40:08.880 --> 00:40:14.420
complex systems can give very, very good performance
but then may not generalize well to data that

00:40:14.420 --> 00:40:17.260
the system has never seen before.

00:40:17.260 --> 00:40:21.910
The advantage, of course, is that you're self-organizing
the data, using these kinds of approaches,

00:40:21.910 --> 00:40:26.940
so there's a potential for new scientific
insights based on what these networks are

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:28.640
actually learning.

00:40:28.640 --> 00:40:33.460
The disadvantage though, of these deep networks
is that they're computationally very intensive,

00:40:33.460 --> 00:40:37.640
and they require highly optimized code, and
often, special processors to be

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:40.730
SPEAKER: Practical for even medium sized data
sets.

00:40:40.730 --> 00:40:46.840
So when you want to train one of these systems
on, for example, the thousands of hours of

00:40:46.840 --> 00:40:54.320
speech data, that I referred to earlier, these
systems can require thousands of processors,

00:40:54.320 --> 00:40:57.390
and can run for days at a time to train.

00:40:57.390 --> 00:41:04.710
So the other, I think, piece of this story
with deep learning is we finally have vast

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:10.020
computational power that's inexpensive enough
that lots of researchers have access to it,

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:13.350
and that's really allow the field to take
off.

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:19.460
The use of special processors such as GPUs
has made these systems computationally feasible,

00:41:19.460 --> 00:41:23.410
and that's one of the reasons why you see
so much work in this area now.

00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:28.240
Finally the compute resources are available
so that these systems can be applied without

00:41:28.240 --> 00:41:33.270
having to have a supercomputer.

00:41:33.270 --> 00:41:39.960
But through this process, I want to come back
to this issue of annotation of data.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:44.890
Unsupervised training is really critical in
this field because you don't really have data

00:41:44.890 --> 00:41:47.460
which is annotated the
way you would really like.

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:52.290
If the world were perfect we'd have lots of
EEG data annotated at the event level, localized

00:41:52.290 --> 00:41:53.310
in time.

00:41:53.310 --> 00:41:54.560
But we never really have that.

00:41:54.560 --> 00:41:57.450
All that we actually have is a report.

00:41:57.450 --> 00:42:00.530
And I'm showing an example of a report here.

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:03.260
These reports are essentially unstructured
text.

00:42:03.260 --> 00:42:10.330
Even in today's EMR, electronic medical records
systems, a lot of the data still aren't structured,

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:13.990
and the language that's used is indirect,
and purposefully defensive.

00:42:13.990 --> 00:42:22.640
So they will say things like, "shows indications
of," rather than stating clearly, yes or no,

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:23.920
patient has a particular syndrome.

00:42:23.920 --> 00:42:27.790
So the report structure has evolved over time.

00:42:27.790 --> 00:42:32.799
Our data that was collected over 14 years
has at least 6 different formats for these

00:42:32.799 --> 00:42:34.869
reports.

00:42:34.869 --> 00:42:37.980
And the temporal events, you'll notice, are
not annotated or localized.

00:42:37.980 --> 00:42:44.220
In fact, in these reports when they say there
is a 10 hertz alpha rhythm, that's actually

00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:45.490
not quite accurate.

00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:49.630
If you actually measure the frequency of the
rhythm it will be different than that.

00:42:49.630 --> 00:42:54.650
This is a observation they make based on a
very crude temporal measurement of the signal.

00:42:54.650 --> 00:42:59.500
So even when they provide numerical data in
these reports there's a good chance that the

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:03.010
data is not actually accurate.

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:06.980
So fully supervised training is not possible
because we just don't have the information

00:43:06.980 --> 00:43:09.700
that we need in these reports to train the
systems.

00:43:09.700 --> 00:43:14.280
This is another big part of the data wrangling
problem, is that you want to be able to build

00:43:14.280 --> 00:43:18.680
systems when you don't have large amounts
of annotated data.

00:43:18.680 --> 00:43:24.329
So what we used in this case is an active
learning approach, where we iteratively are

00:43:24.329 --> 00:43:30.700
able to learn the structure of the data with
very minimal amounts of annotated data.

00:43:30.700 --> 00:43:37.230
We start with a small amount of hand labelled
data that usually comes from an expert.

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:38.950
And then we bootstrap the process.

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:41.450
We take that data, we build some models.

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:43.240
We process the data.

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:49.210
We identify data which we have high confidence
in, data which we have low confidence in,

00:43:49.210 --> 00:43:54.320
and then we adjust the models based on that
data, and go back and retrain.

00:43:54.320 --> 00:44:03.640
And we integrate so that the system gets better
and better at identifying the data that we

00:44:03.640 --> 00:44:05.109
have high confidence in.

00:44:05.109 --> 00:44:09.260
But we're also able to take the data that
we have low confidence in, and use that to

00:44:09.260 --> 00:44:15.900
adjust the performance of the system by teaching
it to learn more about the data that it has

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:17.780
trouble recognizing.

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:20.210
Sometimes that's done through a human intervention.

00:44:20.210 --> 00:44:25.540
Sometimes we have to have an expert
review that data and decide which data is

00:44:25.540 --> 00:44:28.300
within class, and which data is out of class.

00:44:28.300 --> 00:44:30.810
Sometimes we can do that fully automatically.

00:44:30.810 --> 00:44:37.299
But almost all systems have to do some amount
of this if you want to be able to bootstrap

00:44:37.299 --> 00:44:40.700
up to the very large amounts of data that
you'd like to process through the system,

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:45.470
because it's rare that you have thousands
of hours, or tens of thousands of hours of

00:44:45.470 --> 00:44:48.099
data that's fully annotated.

00:44:48.099 --> 00:44:56.020
Now, one other topic I wanted to briefly mention
was that channel normalization, and adaptation

00:44:56.020 --> 00:44:58.840
are also things which are important to this
problem.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:04.510
Some physiological systems, like EEGs are
collected under relatively controlled conditions,

00:45:04.510 --> 00:45:10.810
so you don't have to worry about the channel,
or the nature of the analog data varying that

00:45:10.810 --> 00:45:11.839
much.

00:45:11.839 --> 00:45:18.570
But even within EEGs, depending on the electrodes,
the technician, the electrical wiring systems,

00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:23.540
you can see systematic variations in the data,
and these kinds of variations are very damaging

00:45:23.540 --> 00:45:25.210
to statistical learning systems.

00:45:25.210 --> 00:45:30.170
So we have adaptation approaches that can
do this type of channel normalization and

00:45:30.170 --> 00:45:37.319
adaptation, and we do make use of those so
that we can automatically ingest data and

00:45:37.319 --> 00:45:41.220
be able to process it in a common framework.

00:45:41.220 --> 00:45:46.549
Today we do that using a same type of deep
learning approach that we use for classification.

00:45:46.549 --> 00:45:51.530
We're learning a mapping of the signals from
these different recording environments, we're

00:45:51.530 --> 00:45:55.970
able to map them into such a way that they
can be directly compared.

00:45:55.970 --> 00:46:01.030
And that's another example of a data wrangling
problem that's very challenging is you have

00:46:01.030 --> 00:46:07.590
to be able to normalize your data so that
it all can be evaluated in a common framework.

00:46:07.590 --> 00:46:14.140
So, in summary big data can be transformative.

00:46:14.140 --> 00:46:18.640
There's many areas of bioengineering where
we have somewhat of an artisan mentality.

00:46:18.640 --> 00:46:25.860
We have experts who have been trained by practice,
and they view their craft as being important

00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:29.549
because they've spent many years acquiring
this expertise.

00:46:29.549 --> 00:46:37.309
But we believe that we can improve on this
by providing the kind of input we can generate

00:46:37.309 --> 00:46:40.960
from these statistical modeling systems.

00:46:40.960 --> 00:46:43.540
We can also personalize patient care.

00:46:43.540 --> 00:46:50.170
We can also inform patients and health care
providers with best practices, which are validated

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:52.790
over world wide data resources.

00:46:52.790 --> 00:46:56.569
That's something that's not happening in many
areas of medicine right now.

00:46:56.569 --> 00:47:00.750
We can also impact training and certification
because we can provide students with searchable

00:47:00.750 --> 00:47:02.990
archives of data.

00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:06.760
Deep learning can play an important role in
this but of course data wrangling becomes

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:12.430
critical because that's the only way we can
create the large data sets that are needed

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:15.619
to be able to train and develop
these large models.

00:47:15.619 --> 00:47:22.619
And robust parsing of unstructured data is
critical, as I mentioned, since diagnoses

00:47:22.619 --> 00:47:25.039
and reports are largely entered as unstructured
text.

00:47:25.039 --> 00:47:30.240
So the companion talk that will be on December
2nd is going to deal with natural language

00:47:30.240 --> 00:47:36.640
processing of these medical reports, and how
we actually are able to bring structure to

00:47:36.640 --> 00:47:39.440
these reports so that we can use it to inform.

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:42.220
the training process.

00:47:42.220 --> 00:47:46.670
But ultimately even when you develop these
kinds of systems it's really important that

00:47:46.670 --> 00:47:51.340
the technology positively impacts quality
of care, efficiency, and costs.

00:47:51.340 --> 00:47:55.839
So we still have to spend a lot of time working
with health care providers to understand how

00:47:55.839 --> 00:47:59.940
they actually make decisions and how they
use this technology in making those decisions.

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:02.390
And that's still a very important piece of
this.

00:48:02.390 --> 00:48:09.500
As I mentioned, with our EEG project, we're
now in our fourth generation of what we thought

00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:12.020
neurologists need, in terms of a decision
support tool.

00:48:12.020 --> 00:48:15.060
And I think now we're getting onto something
which is really useful.

00:48:15.060 --> 00:48:19.400
But it was very hard to initially talk to
them, and get that information.

00:48:19.400 --> 00:48:23.800
It required working closely with them, over
a long period of time, trying to understand

00:48:23.800 --> 00:48:28.309
exactly how they do their work, and what the
gaps were, and what the technology gaps were

00:48:28.309 --> 00:48:31.050
in that work.

00:48:31.050 --> 00:48:34.510
So with that, I think I'll open the floor
to questions.

00:48:34.510 --> 00:48:41.119
SPEAKER: So Dr. PIcone, thank you so much
for such a clear and wonderful talk, which

00:48:41.119 --> 00:48:42.799
was incredibly informative.

00:48:42.799 --> 00:48:47.789
We do have quite a few questions already that
have come in.

00:48:47.789 --> 00:48:54.180
The first one that I want to pose to you is
about how might a patient donate EEG data

00:48:54.180 --> 00:48:55.380
to your database?

00:48:55.380 --> 00:49:03.450
But more generally, what difficulties, if
any, may arise from bringing in data from

00:49:03.450 --> 00:49:08.369
other sources, whether it's donated from across
the country, or whether you're bringing in

00:49:08.369 --> 00:49:15.579
another set from a wholesale, from another
hospital system?

00:49:15.579 --> 00:49:16.750
JOSEPH PICONE: Yes.

00:49:16.750 --> 00:49:19.280
I have the same questions.

00:49:19.280 --> 00:49:23.850
So, we have been trying to get hold of data
for EEGs, and that's been a very interesting

00:49:23.850 --> 00:49:30.000
process because a lot of institutions are
very protective of their data for a variety

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:32.770
of reasons, including HIPPA type issues.

00:49:32.770 --> 00:49:39.260
We are in the process of actually building
a seizure detection subset of this data, that's

00:49:39.260 --> 00:49:44.170
going to be annotated by multiple neurologists,
and is going to include data from multiple

00:49:44.170 --> 00:49:45.170
sites.

00:49:45.170 --> 00:49:48.020
So we're going to be able to answer those
questions in a much more detailed way probably

00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:51.160
within about 3 to 4 months.

00:49:51.160 --> 00:49:55.200
We believe signal like EEG, from what I've
seen, it's collected

00:49:55.200 --> 00:50:00.619
under fairly controlled conditions, and most
hospitals use 1 of 2 systems.

00:50:00.619 --> 00:50:03.950
So there's not a lot of variation in the way
this data is collected.

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:08.050
But with other types of applications, like
digital pathology, for example, there's a

00:50:08.050 --> 00:50:12.010
big variation in the way slides are prepared
and digitized.

00:50:12.010 --> 00:50:14.240
And that's one of the reasons we want to work
in that area next.

00:50:14.240 --> 00:50:19.119
We really want to understand how we can do
data wrangling when the data is much more

00:50:19.119 --> 00:50:21.660
variable from hospital to hospital.

00:50:21.660 --> 00:50:26.490
SPEAKER: It sounds like there are a lot of
challenges there.

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:31.440
So the next questions are about deep learning
in particular.

00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:35.359
Let me start with the one the more general
ones.

00:50:35.359 --> 00:50:40.980
How would you define or characterize deep
learning machine learning systems, and contrast

00:50:40.980 --> 00:50:45.539
them with other types of machine learning,
apart from their need for big data?

00:50:45.539 --> 00:50:55.400
JOSEPH PICONE: Yeah, that's a-- so there are,
I think if we look at the space of machine

00:50:55.400 --> 00:51:04.349
learning there are, of course, parametric
models, non-parametric models, there are models

00:51:04.349 --> 00:51:10.710
that have a small number of parameters, and
there's models that have large numbers of

00:51:10.710 --> 00:51:11.710
parameters.

00:51:11.710 --> 00:51:17.329
I would tend to call deep learning systems
that are architected as directed graphs of

00:51:17.329 --> 00:51:20.619
very simple computational units.

00:51:20.619 --> 00:51:27.580
So those units could be simple things like
a weighted sum of its inputs, followed by

00:51:27.580 --> 00:51:28.580
some kind of non-linearity.

00:51:28.580 --> 00:51:32.809
That's the classic artificial neural network.

00:51:32.809 --> 00:51:38.170
But you have lots of these very simple nodes.

00:51:38.170 --> 00:51:44.790
In the past, with neural networks in particular,
when we had 3 layer networks, those were fairly

00:51:44.790 --> 00:51:49.470
well known, and those existed before deep
learning took off.

00:51:49.470 --> 00:51:53.420
When people talk about deep learning today
they're talking about networks that have far

00:51:53.420 --> 00:51:59.211
more than 3 layers, and have millions of parameters
associated with them.

00:51:59.211 --> 00:52:00.880
And I think that's the big distinction.

00:52:00.880 --> 00:52:05.680
We had what were called multi-layer perceptrons
many years ago, but they were simply 3 layer

00:52:05.680 --> 00:52:06.680
networks.

00:52:06.680 --> 00:52:12.569
Totally general in what they could learn,
but they were not as many levels, or as deep,

00:52:12.569 --> 00:52:16.730
vertically so to speak, as the systems that
are being trained today.

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:26.039
SPEAKER: And I also just want to add to the
person who asked the question that in January--

00:52:26.039 --> 00:52:31.210
well no, not January, but after The New Year,
so maybe more like February, we'll have a

00:52:31.210 --> 00:52:37.130
couple of talks on machine learning in general
to layout that big picture of machine learning,

00:52:37.130 --> 00:52:43.750
with the supervised, and unsupervised learning,
and classification, and regression type problems.

00:52:43.750 --> 00:52:51.100
So we'll will see this again, and more in
a machine learning more holistically, later

00:52:51.100 --> 00:52:52.100
on.

00:52:52.100 --> 00:52:53.630
JOSEPH PICONE: Excellent.

00:52:53.630 --> 00:52:54.900
Yeah, excellent.

00:52:54.900 --> 00:53:03.050
SPEAKER: Another question is about the mechanics
of the deep learning.

00:53:03.050 --> 00:53:15.000
And the questioner asks about how sensitive
the gradient descent is to the initial starting

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:26.890
point, but in addition, how sensitive is it
to the different variations of gradient descent

00:53:26.890 --> 00:53:27.890
that are out there?

00:53:27.890 --> 00:53:31.940
There are all different types of optimization
we could be using.

00:53:31.940 --> 00:53:41.110
Some of which are small tweaks, some of which
are more even more different, but you know,

00:53:41.110 --> 00:53:47.319
how sensitive is it to all those different
choices that could be made in order to the

00:53:47.319 --> 00:53:48.319
deep learning model?

00:53:48.319 --> 00:53:50.480
JOSEPH PICONE: Very, very good question.

00:53:50.480 --> 00:53:59.170
So I'll start with a general comment, which
is you don't just pick up a tool kit off the

00:53:59.170 --> 00:54:02.319
net, and implement deep learning, and get
state of the art performance.

00:54:02.319 --> 00:54:07.250
The reality is there's a lot of heuristics,
a lot of anecdotal information, a lot of tweaking

00:54:07.250 --> 00:54:13.190
that goes on for these state of the art systems
to deliver the performance that they do.

00:54:13.190 --> 00:54:18.000
Most of our work is based on a open-source
tool kit, Theano for example, and we can run

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:23.480
a lot of the packages built within that tool,
and these packages tend to perform comparably

00:54:23.480 --> 00:54:24.770
on the data that we have.

00:54:24.770 --> 00:54:28.650
But once you start getting deep into the research,
and tweaking them, you can make them do a

00:54:28.650 --> 00:54:30.020
little bit better.

00:54:30.020 --> 00:54:36.089
With regard to gradient descent, and type,
I was formerly of the impression that there

00:54:36.089 --> 00:54:37.410
wasn't much difference.

00:54:37.410 --> 00:54:41.910
We had seen comparable performance across
a lot of different configurations.

00:54:41.910 --> 00:54:49.589
But recently with these LSTM networks we have
been seeing some significant improvement in

00:54:49.589 --> 00:54:54.410
performance using a particular set of training
techniques, and I can talk offline about what

00:54:54.410 --> 00:54:55.420
those are.

00:54:55.420 --> 00:54:59.260
But generally you're talking about a 10% relative
reduction in error rate.

00:54:59.260 --> 00:55:08.059
So our system goes from about 70% accuracy
to 80% accuracy using what you might say are

00:55:08.059 --> 00:55:11.170
best practices, in terms of training.

00:55:11.170 --> 00:55:14.440
That is, in some ways, you could say substantial.

00:55:14.440 --> 00:55:18.920
But the problem with both those systems is
the false alarm rate is still 2 orders of

00:55:18.920 --> 00:55:20.720
magnitude higher than it needs to be.

00:55:20.720 --> 00:55:25.690
So that's sort of in the noise compared to
the problem we have with false alarm rates.

00:55:25.690 --> 00:55:30.450
SPEAKER: OK, but still some improvement is
better than nothing.

00:55:30.450 --> 00:55:32.079
So-
JOSEPH PICONE: Some improvement's better than

00:55:32.079 --> 00:55:35.700
nothing, and the science in this area is getting
much better.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:41.200
But there's still a lot of art associated
with training these deep learning systems.

00:55:41.200 --> 00:55:50.280
SPEAKER: Yes, and so that's why we need experts
to do this, and to help with this.

00:55:50.280 --> 00:55:54.670
JOSEPH PICONE: And by the way, if anybody
wants to contact us, we'll be happy to share

00:55:54.670 --> 00:55:58.950
everything we're
doing, and help bring you up to speed on what

00:55:58.950 --> 00:56:01.230
we think works well for these kinds of signals.

00:56:01.230 --> 00:56:02.660
SPEAKER: Thank you.

00:56:02.660 --> 00:56:05.760
I'm sure people will take you up on that.

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:10.800
So there is a question about-- let me just
ask it.

00:56:10.800 --> 00:56:15.059
Do you see your model and algorithms being
used in other fields?

00:56:15.059 --> 00:56:21.670
I also was thinking about this as you were
talking, because I find it very interesting

00:56:21.670 --> 00:56:27.140
how you took the state of the art from the
speech community, and brought in the ideas

00:56:27.140 --> 00:56:31.980
about using the frequency domain, in addition
to time domain, and applied them in biomedical

00:56:31.980 --> 00:56:32.980
science.

00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:39.130
And I was wondering how do you see what you're
doing now, in the EEG and biomedical science

00:56:39.130 --> 00:56:44.730
world, do you see any of that being translated
back into other domains, like a more general

00:56:44.730 --> 00:56:45.840
speech recognition?

00:56:45.840 --> 00:56:48.710
JOSEPH PICONE: I would hope so.

00:56:48.710 --> 00:56:55.360
We did some work on non-parametric Bayesian
in models that has been cross-fertilizing

00:56:55.360 --> 00:56:59.789
in both domains.

00:56:59.789 --> 00:57:03.930
I'd like to say yes, but the problem is, the
amount of investment in areas like speech

00:57:03.930 --> 00:57:07.980
and image is so great, and you've got some
of the best groups in the world putting tremendous

00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:12.260
amounts of resources in those problems, so
it's been a little bit difficult to keep up

00:57:12.260 --> 00:57:16.180
with that in a smaller area like EEG processing.

00:57:16.180 --> 00:57:21.920
But the techniques we're using on EEG are
informed by speech and image recognition.

00:57:21.920 --> 00:57:24.420
So there's no reason it couldn't go the other
way.

00:57:24.420 --> 00:57:29.930
We try not to develop techniques that are
specific to 1 application.

00:57:29.930 --> 00:57:33.480
And that's why, for example, we're going to
take the same technology and apply it to digital

00:57:33.480 --> 00:57:34.480
pathology.

00:57:34.480 --> 00:57:36.441
And I think that we will have some success
there as well.

00:57:36.441 --> 00:57:42.150
SPEAKER: Well this is very exciting, and a
very exciting area.

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:49.890
We do really look forward to the [INAUDIBLE]
talk in a couple of weeks.

00:57:49.890 --> 00:57:59.630
I think we should go ahead and wrap it up
today, and let everyone know that we can talk

00:57:59.630 --> 00:58:04.410
more about it in 2 weeks from now, after Thanksgiving.

00:58:04.410 --> 00:58:10.890
So Dr. Picone, I want to thank you so much
for taking the time today to tell us about

00:58:10.890 --> 00:58:17.069
this work, and to tell us about data wrangling,
and give us a big picture of it.

00:58:17.069 --> 00:58:24.791
And I really look forward to hearing your
colleague speak about the text mining, in

00:58:24.791 --> 00:58:25.791
2 weeks.

00:58:25.791 --> 00:58:29.940
So thank you, and thank you everybody for
joining us today.

00:58:29.940 --> 00:58:30.940
Bye bye.

00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:31.940
JOSEPH PICONE: Thank you.

